    tags: ["Aadhaar","Privacy","Mask Aadhaar","RBI KYC","Cyber Security","Identity"],
    content: "# Masked Aadhaar vs Regular Aadhaar: RBI Master Direction for Hotel Check-ins, KYC & Cyber Safety (2026)\n\nEvery time you hand over an unmasked photocopy of your Aadhaar card to a hotel receptionist, building security guard, bike rental agency, or courier delivery personnel, you expose yourself to identity theft, unauthorized bank SIM swaps, and fraudulent loan applications. Over 80% of cyber crimes reported to the National Cyber Crime Reporting Portal ([cybercrime.gov.in](https://cybercrime.gov.in)) trace back to compromised physical photocopies discarded in unsecured commercial filing cabinets. To combat widespread identity harvesting, the Unique Identification Authority of India ([uidai.gov.in](https://uidai.gov.in)) and the Reserve Bank of India (RBI) officially introduced and mandated **Masked Aadhaar**. Learn the legal differences, your rights as a citizen, and how to redact your card in seconds using Kagazo's free [Mask Aadhaar](https://kagazo.in/tools/mask-aadhaar) tool.\n\n## Key Takeaways\n\n- **What Is Masked Aadhaar?** A legally valid version of your Aadhaar where the first 8 digits are redacted as `XXXX-XXXX-`, leaving only the last 4 digits visible alongside your photo, name, and demographic details.\n- **Supreme Court & UIDAI Ruling:** Under the 2018 Supreme Court constitutional bench judgment (*Justice K.S. Puttaswamy*), private entities are prohibited from demanding or storing your full 12-digit Aadhaar number unless authorized by law.\n- **RBI Master Direction on KYC:** Regulated financial entities (banks, NBFCs, payment wallets) are statutorily required under Section 16 of the RBI Master Direction to mask the first 8 digits before storing any customer Aadhaar record.\n- **Hotels Cannot Demand Full Aadhaar:** The Ministry of Tourism and UIDAI circulars confirm that hotels and guest houses cannot insist on unmasked Aadhaar; presenting a Masked Aadhaar with photo is 100% legally sufficient for identity verification.\n- **In-Browser Redaction:** Kagazo masks Aadhaar cards entirely inside client-side browser RAM memory; your biometric barcode and demographic data never touch an external server.\n\n---\n\n## Regular Aadhaar vs. Masked Aadhaar Comparison\n\n| Feature | Regular Aadhaar | Masked Aadhaar (Kagazo Sovereign) |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Visible Digits** | All 12 Digits (`1234 5678 9012`) | Last 4 Digits Only (`XXXX XXXX 9012`) |\n| **QR Barcode** | Full demographic payload | Secure offline verification payload |\n| **Legal Validity** | 100% Valid everywhere | 100% Valid for KYC, Hotels, Sim Cards |\n| **SIM Swap Risk** | Extremely High if copy leaked | Zero Risk (Telecoms require full digits) |\n| **Hotel Acceptance** | Mandatory to accept | Statutorily mandated by Ministry of Tourism |\n| **RBI KYC Compliance** | Banks must mask before storage | Pre-masked for zero banking compliance friction |\n\n---\n\n## Why Sharing Full Aadhaar Photocopies Is Dangerous\n\n**Leaking full 12-digit Aadhaar numbers enables fraudsters to pull credit reports, initiate Aadhaar-Enabled Payment System (AePS) exploits, and obtain fraudulent telecom connections.**\n\n1. **Aadhaar-Enabled Payment System (AePS) Fraud:** If a fraudster obtains your 12-digit Aadhaar number and copies your latent silicone fingerprint from a property deed or cyber café scanner, they can siphon funds from your linked bank account via AePS micro-ATMs.\n2. **SIM Swap Syndicates:** Unscrupulous telecom retailers use unmasked copies to issue duplicate SIM cards in your name, hijacking bank two-factor SMS OTPs.\n3. **Fake Rental Agreements & Shell Companies:** Scammers use stolen copies to register shell GST entities or lease commercial properties for illicit operations.\n\n---\n\n## What Does the Law Say About Private Entities Demanding Aadhaar?\n\nUnder **Section 29 of the Aadhaar (Targeted Delivery of Financial and Other Subsidies, Benefits and Services) Act, 2016**:\n- No core biometric information or full Aadhaar number shall be published, displayed, or shared publicly.\n- Any individual or entity that violates Section 29 is punishable with imprisonment for a term which may extend to **three years or a fine up to ₹1,00,000**, or both.\n\nUnder the **RBI Master Direction – Know Your Customer (KYC) Direction, 2016 (Updated 2025)**:\n> *\"Where the customer submits an officially valid document containing the Aadhaar number, the regulated entity shall redact or blackout the Aadhaar number through appropriate measures before storing.\"*\n\n---\n\n## How to Mask Your Aadhaar Card in 5 Seconds on Kagazo\n\n1. **Open the Tool:** Navigate to [Mask Aadhaar](https://kagazo.in/tools/mask-aadhaar).\n2. **Upload Card Image or PDF:** Drag your e-Aadhaar PDF or smartphone camera photo into the secure workspace.\n3. **Enter Password (If PDF):** For official UIDAI PDFs, provide the standard 8-character password (`FIRST4NAME` + `YEAROFBIRTH`).\n4. **Automatic Blackout:** Kagazo detects the 12-digit bounding box and applies permanent monochrome black redaction over the first 8 digits.\n5. **Download Masked Card:** Save your print-ready JPEG or single-page PDF. You can now present this copy to hotels, airports, and security desks with total peace of mind.",
